The '''Third Republic period (1947–1968)''' covers the post-war reconstruction of Guajicutea, the establishment of the Third Republic under a new constitution, and the Labor Party's governance through the reconstruction and tourism boom of the 1950s and 1960s. The period began with the liberation of July 3, 1947, when the last Volisanian forces withdrew following the overthrow of the [[National Volisan Party|NVP]] in [[Volisania]], and ended in 1968 with the election of a conservative president.

== Liberation and the transitional government ==

Following the overthrow of the NVP in [[Volisania]] in 1947, the last Volisanian forces withdrew from Guajicutea on July 3. A group of conservative-moderate leaders who had been retained during the war — referred to as the rump government — assumed governance. Weeks of domestic unrest followed, including political violence and food shortages.

On August 7, 1947, the rump government requested a coalition occupation by [[Balisca|Baliscan]] forces to restore order. Baliscan forces occupied the island for six months, during which they worked to restore civil order, release political prisoners from the [[Campo de Las Minas]] and other detention facilities, and re-establish food supply. [[Asikyira]] sent aid during the occupation, including food, medical supplies, and reconstruction assistance.

A unified coalition of non-fascist factions, the [[Partido de la Restauración Guajicuteana]] (PRG), governed the transitional period from August 7 to December 18, 1947. The PRG was led by [[Germán Wakurí]], a former [[Grupo de Liberación Guajicuteano|GLG]] freedom fighter. The transitional government was assisted by [[Adonian Community|AC]] consultants from [[Zong]], [[Volisania]], and [[Burawa]] during the creation of the constitution.

== The Tribunales de Liberación ==

Fascists were arrested and tried in the [[Tribunales de Liberación]] (Liberation Tribunals), conducted during the transitional period. Many were sentenced to death or life in prison.

== The constitution (December 18, 1947) ==

On December 18, 1947, a new constitution was promulgated, establishing the '''Third Republic of Guajicutea'''. The constitution included the following guarantees and provisions:

# '''Full enfranchisement''' — universal suffrage.
# '''Freedom of speech, protest, assembly, and press''' — fundamental civil liberties.
# '''Prevention of presidential usurption''' — constitutional mechanisms to prevent a repeat of the [[Jefe de la Asamblea]] subversion.
# '''Restriction of foreign ownership''' — national institutions, whether public or private, cannot be owned by foreign entities.
# '''Religious protection''' — freedom of religion.
# '''Language protection''' — no national language. Common languages are Guaji and Sartan, both taught to all.
# '''Right to education''' — universal right to education for all.
# '''Citizenship''' — all pre-war residents are granted citizenship, regardless of race or origin.
# '''Military service''' — mandatory 2-year service program for all 18-year-olds.
# '''District restructuring''' — the 16 districtos with Sartan colonial names imposed in 1609 were renamed. The colonial saint names were dropped in favour of the pre-colonial Guaji names: San Mateo became [[Distrito de Guanaran]], Altavista became [[Distrito de Barikan]], and so forth. The [[Warikú]] island, joined as a separate 17th district. Boundaries were otherwise unchanged.

== The Adonian Community and reparations ==

On January 1, 1948, the [[Adonian Community]] (AC) was established to prevent future conflicts. Guajicutea was invited as an initial member. The AC negotiated Volisanian reparations to Guajicutea for reconstruction. The reparations were spread over approximately ten years (1948–1958) and covered the reconstruction of [[Waribá]], food imports, industrial rebuilding, infrastructure repair, and museum and cultural restoration. The [[Partido Laborista de Guajicutea|Labor Party]]'s strategy was to invest reparations funds in building sustainable economics rather than consuming them on short-term relief.

== First election (January 18, 1948) ==

The first election under the new constitution was held on January 18, 1948. The [[Partido Laborista de Guajicutea]] (PLG) — the Labor Party, formed from the non-fascist coalition — won 80% of assembly seats. The [[Partido del Progreso de Guaji]] (PPG) — the Progress for Guaji Party, moderate and conservative — placed second. PLG leader [[Germán Wakurí]] became the first president of the Third Republic.

The assembly was apportioned at one representative per 10,000 residents. With a population of approximately 490,000 (per the 1950 census, conducted the same day), the first assembly had approximately 49 seats. Assembly elections were held every two years; presidential elections every four years. Elected officials took office in November of the election year.

== Reconstruction ==

=== Waribá rebuild ===

[[Waribá]], mostly destroyed during the war by firebombing in 1945, was rebuilt over approximately eight years (~1948–~1956). The city was reoriented toward hospitality and specialty agriculture. Post-war peace produced a growing class of travellers in [[Illypnia]] and [[Kaftia]] eager to visit the new world, and Guajicutea capitalised on this demand. Waribá transitioned to a hospitality industry, with nature and historical restoration, and the construction of large museums as part of the cultural reconstruction. A baby boom accompanied the reconstruction, and Waribá's population grew faster than its infrastructure could accommodate.

=== Waku Bá ===

A new city, [[Waku Bá]], was planned and built in the northwest of Guajicutea on the site of a destroyed Volisanian navy port. The city was constructed alongside the Waribá reconstruction, with large sprawling suburbs, transit connections, and apartment blocks with courtyards designed for family life. Waku Bá grew rapidly as the baby boom and reconstruction drew population to the northwest.

=== Pro-natalist policies ===

The Labor government instituted pro-natalist policies to promote population growth. Financial reimbursement covered childbirth costs, hospital fees, and postnatal care. Free housing was provided to larger families and married couples, with the option to upgrade to larger homes for each additional child. Housing in Waribá and Waku Bá was built with family-friendly urban planning: large sprawling suburbs, transit-connected, with apartment blocks with courtyards. The housing program served a dual purpose — supporting the growing population and providing construction employment.

=== Economic strategy ===

High employment in construction characterised the first years. The Labor Party invested reparations funds in building sustainable economics. The [[Ministry of Development|Minister of Development]] [[Óscar Gallardo]] played a central role, aligning Waribá toward hospitality and specialty agriculture and directing investment toward sustainable industries.

== Demographics ==

The population of Guajicutea grew from approximately 453,000 in 1947 to approximately 490,000 in 1950, a growth rate of approximately 2.5% per year, driven by the baby boom and reconstruction.

